# Branch discipline

This rule is an opt-in reusable profile. It is not the public contribution
policy of this repository. Apply it only when a derived project explicitly
adopts strict issue-numbered branches.

Keep Git branches focused, traceable, and limited to one coherent unit of
work.

## Branch scope

- Every meaningful change MUST belong to an appropriately scoped branch.
- A branch MUST represent one coherent issue, feature, bug fix, improvement,
  documentation change, or other independently reviewable unit of work.
- Do not accumulate unrelated work on a branch merely because that branch is
  already checked out.
- Do not treat a change as belonging to the current branch merely because it
  exists in the working tree.
- Before staging or committing a change, verify that it genuinely belongs to
  the purpose and scope of the current branch.
- If a discovered or requested change does not belong to the current branch,
  keep it separate and handle it through an appropriate branch or separately
  approved workflow.
- Incidental cleanup, refactoring, formatting, documentation, or fixes that
  are unrelated to the branch purpose MUST NOT be bundled into the branch
  unless they are necessary for the intended change.
- Preserve unrelated pre-existing working-tree changes. Do not stage, modify,
  revert, or commit them as part of the current branch.

## Commit scope

- Commits on a branch MUST contain only changes relevant to that branch's
  purpose.
- Before committing, inspect the staged diff and exclude unrelated files or
  hunks.
- Prefer coherent commits that represent understandable steps within the
  branch's overall purpose.
- Do not use broad staging such as `git add .` or an equivalent command when
  it could include unrelated changes without first verifying exactly what
  will be staged.
- A commit MUST NOT be used as a convenient container for unrelated
  outstanding work.
- If a file contains both branch-related and unrelated changes, stage only
  the relevant hunks when this can be done safely.
- Implementation approval does not imply approval to commit, create, rename,
  delete, merge, rebase, or otherwise mutate branches. Follow the repository's
  applicable Git authorization workflow.

## Branch naming

Use this format:

```text
<type>/<number>-<short-name>
```

The numeric component is mandatory. It MUST NOT be omitted, replaced by a
non-numeric identifier, or treated as optional under any circumstance covered
by this rule.

The allowed branch types are:

- `feat` - new functionality or behavior;
- `bugfix` - an ordinary bug fix;
- `hotfix` - an urgent production fix requiring expedited handling;
- `imp` - an improvement to existing behavior or implementation;
- `refactor` - a structural change without intended behavioral change;
- `perf` - a performance improvement;
- `test` - test-only work;
- `docs` - documentation-only work;
- `build` - dependency, packaging, or build-system work;
- `ci` - CI/CD or pipeline work;
- `chore` - maintenance work that does not fit a more specific type.

### Numbering policy

- When an issue or task number is available, the branch number MUST be that
  issue or task number.
- Do not replace a known issue/task number with a locally generated sequence
  number.
- If no issue/task number is available or has been provided, a fallback
  incremental number MUST be allocated.
- The fallback sequence is maintained independently for each branch type.
- The first fallback candidate for a branch type is `1`.
- Each later fallback candidate MUST be exactly one greater than the highest
  fallback number previously allocated for that same branch type.
- If a candidate is already used by any branch of the same type, including an
  issue- or task-based branch, advance to the first higher unused number.
- An available fallback candidate MUST NOT be skipped intentionally.
- A fallback number MUST NEVER be reused for another branch of the same type,
  even if the earlier branch was merged, renamed, closed, or deleted.
- A fallback allocation MUST NOT reuse a numeric component already used by
  another branch of the same type, regardless of how that number originated.
- The same fallback numeric value MAY exist under different branch types
  because each type has an independent fallback sequence.
- Before allocating a fallback number, inspect the repository's relevant
  branch history, remote/local refs, or other established branch registry to
  determine both the fallback sequence and the numeric components already
  used by that branch type.
- Do not guess a fallback number when the existing sequence cannot be
  established reliably.
- If neither an issue/task number nor a reliable fallback number can be
  determined, stop and surface the uncertainty rather than creating an
  unnumbered or potentially duplicate branch.
- The fallback number exists only to preserve mandatory branch numbering when
  no issue/task number is available. It MUST NOT be presented as an issue/task
  identifier.

Branch names MUST:

- use one of the allowed branch types;
- include the mandatory numeric component;
- use the related issue/task number when one is available;
- use a fallback incremental number only when no issue/task number is
  available;
- use a short, descriptive, lowercase kebab-case name;
- avoid spaces, underscores, uppercase characters, and unnecessary wording.

Unless the repository defines a stricter naming policy, branch names SHOULD
match:

```regex
^(feat|bugfix|hotfix|imp|refactor|perf|test|docs|build|ci|chore)/[1-9][0-9]*-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$
```

Examples with issue/task numbers:

```text
feat/123-add-market-order
bugfix/456-fix-fee-calculation
hotfix/789-restore-withdrawals
imp/321-improve-order-validation
refactor/852-split-payment-service
perf/741-reduce-order-query-count
test/963-cover-settlement-flow
docs/654-update-api-guide
build/151-update-ruff
ci/160-add-security-check
chore/171-clean-dev-scripts
```

Fallback examples when no issue/task number is available:

```text
feat/1-add-market-order
feat/2-add-stop-limit
bugfix/1-fix-fee-calculation
bugfix/2-fix-balance-rounding
```

## Existing repository policy

- An explicit repository or organization Git or branch policy takes
  precedence over this rule.
- Public contributors to this boilerplate follow `CONTRIBUTING.md`; they are
  not required to use this optional numbering scheme.
- If the repository already defines allowed branch types, naming rules, issue
  requirements, or branch-protection requirements, follow those instead of
  introducing a competing convention.
- A more specific compatible repository policy may narrow this rule.
- If an applicable repository policy materially conflicts with this rule,
  surface the conflict rather than silently overriding the repository.
